WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:07.390
I am Meghan Hall.

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:10.900
I am going to be speaking today
on the experience of being

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.090
the only R user in my workplace.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:15.130
I'm so excited to
give this talk.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:18.910
I'm sorry I am not there
in person as planned,

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:21.460
but I do have to
give a special thanks

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:24.220
to the conference organizer

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:27.250
who definitely handled my
very last minute switch

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:30.070
to presenting virtually instead.

00:00:30.070 --> 00:00:31.960
The team I suppose
formerly known

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.400
as the RStudio, created these
great talk graphics for us.

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:40.120
But there is not quite
enough self promotion for my taste,

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.543
so feel free to
follow me on Twitter.

00:00:42.543 --> 00:00:43.960
But really, just
want to thank you

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:46.300
all so much for
watching my talk and I

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:48.070
want to give kind
of a special shout

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:51.675
out welcome to anyone who
might be watching virtually.

00:00:51.675 --> 00:00:53.050
Maybe watching
this recording

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:54.470
when it's posted later.

00:00:54.470 --> 00:00:57.250
Maybe someone who's even
a little earlier in there

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:58.090
R journey.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:01.060
And because this
talk is also for you.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:04.360
There should be time at the
end of talk for questions,

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:06.580
if any come up
while I'm speaking.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:10.690
You can put them at
Slido with the hashtag,

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:14.110
or I'll also be around the
Discord server this afternoon

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:19.130
if there's any topic that you
want to chat further about.

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:21.260
So this is my laptop.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.350
It looks like a lot of
your laptops out there.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.690
But to me, this is the
most special laptop

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:29.540
of all, because
this laptop is why

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.720
I learned are to set
the stage a little bit.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:34.910
I've been working
as a data professional

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.100
for over seven years in
higher ed administration.

00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.980
I currently work at Brown
University as a data manager

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.350
and in that role
I am really kind

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:48.750
of the data person in a
functional business office.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.450
I don't work in
I.T., I don't work

00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:55.370
on a data team, a lot of other
data fluent people around me.

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:59.417
And as you can imagine,
my [inaudible] is Excel mostly.

00:01:59.417 --> 00:02:01.250
And so kind of at the
beginning of my career

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:04.010
I was also using Excel
for my data analysis

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:08.690
because again, that was the
atmosphere that I was in.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:12.440
And then a few years later I
started getting into hockey

00:02:12.440 --> 00:02:16.490
and because I am a
numbers person, whatever

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:19.550
other euphemism you
prefer to use for nerd,

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.440
I pretty quickly
started gravitating

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:24.050
to the analytics
side of the game,

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.110
started kind of getting involved
in the public sports analytics

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:28.010
community.

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:31.370
And as I started embarking
on my own analysis,

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:34.190
I started using
Excel because that

00:02:34.190 --> 00:02:36.620
is what I was most
recently familiar with.

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:39.440
So what I used to work for a
lot of the data analysis work

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.810
that I. But let me tell
you, this lovely laptop

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:47.590
that you see here that I
still have and still this 2015

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:51.040
MacBook Air could not
handle Excel files that

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.380
had over a million
just didn't work.

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:57.400
Crash I couldn't even filter
couldn't even do basic stuff.

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.610
So out of technical
necessity, I started learning.

00:03:01.610 --> 00:03:07.900
R. I had been somewhat [iaudible] in R 
and SAS and Stata from grad school

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:10.870
but decided to pursue
learning because truly it

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:13.510
seemed like it had the
most welcoming and kind

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:15.850
of inclusive community and
it seemed like something that

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:18.310
was really learnable to me.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:21.560
And so an experience that many of
you are probably familiar with.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:24.100
I became much happier when
I was doing my data analysis

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:28.300
with as is much more suited to
that type of data analysis work

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.650
than Excel is.

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:31.870
But again, even though
I was very happy

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.240
using R for the sports
analytics work

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:36.850
that I was doing on the side,
it took me a little while

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:41.500
to incorporate all of that R
knowledge into my work,

00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:45.130
and the work I was doing for
money because again, as I said,

00:03:45.130 --> 00:03:47.510
no one around me was using R.

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:50.080
So it didn't seem a very
welcoming environment

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.660
to start using it.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:55.300
So I realized I don't need to
spend a lot of time preaching

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:56.540
to this crowd.

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:58.480
That R is great
because we all know

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:01.810
that R is a really
ideal tool for dealing

00:04:01.810 --> 00:04:03.850
with reproducible data analysis.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:07.210
Right and one of the things that
is kind of magical and amazing

00:04:07.210 --> 00:04:11.680
about R is that you can use R
for your entire data analysis

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:13.180
workflow.

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.790
But I would argue that
kind of equally amazing

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:19.810
is you don't have to use R
for your entire data analysis

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:20.620
workflow.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:22.300
That might work for
a lot of people,

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:24.260
but it might not
work for everyone.

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:25.780
Might not work for
you if it doesn't

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:30.910
fit within the constraints you
might have upon you at work.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:32.830
And again, I think
it's I would argue

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:35.740
that it's like equally great
kind of an equal strength of R

00:04:35.740 --> 00:04:39.897
that even if you just kind
of use bits and pieces of R,

00:04:39.897 --> 00:04:41.980
incorporate them into your
work, that really helps

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:43.810
solve your specific problems.

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:48.220
You don't need to use the entire
again, A to Z workflow of R

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.300
in order to get
those benefits of R.

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.770
And so you can really just focus
on what is possible for you

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:57.670
and what helps you
because it's for sure true

00:04:57.670 --> 00:05:00.700
that there are struggles
to incorporating R

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:04.840
into a workflow that might not
feel very welcoming toward it.

00:05:04.840 --> 00:05:07.450
But I'm going to today I'm
going to talk about some

00:05:07.450 --> 00:05:08.950
of the struggles
that I have faced

00:05:08.950 --> 00:05:11.950
going through that process, how
I've handled those struggles,

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:15.100
and how I still think they
are greatly outweighed

00:05:15.100 --> 00:05:18.760
by the benefits you get of using
R again, even if you can only

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:23.410
use bits and pieces of
the entire R ecosystem.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:26.390
So I hope that this talk will
be helpful and inspiring to you.

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:28.900
Whether you are you
yourself are starting

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:31.660
to think about using
at work, whether you're

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:35.645
like me and a lone
wolf, using artwork

00:05:35.645 --> 00:05:37.270
in trying to kind of
spread the gospel,

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:40.250
I'm looking for some tips about
how to make that job easier.

00:05:40.250 --> 00:05:44.170
Or maybe even you're involved
in helping usher other people

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:49.270
along similar journeys along
how to, how to incorporate R

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:50.330
into their workflow.

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:54.440
So hopefully this will
give you some tips on that.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:56.960
So in addition to my
work in higher ed,

00:05:56.960 --> 00:06:00.230
I also spent some time at
Zelus Analytics, which

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:02.210
is a sports analytics company.

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.970
And there I worked
as a data scientist

00:06:04.970 --> 00:06:07.670
among a team of
other data scientists

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:11.000
in kind of a software
development team.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:15.480
And there, my entire workflow
that I worked in was in R.

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:17.900
I was able to, within R,

00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.080
write MySQL code directly
connect to the databases

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:23.210
to pull the data that I needed.

00:06:23.210 --> 00:06:26.780
The products I created were in
R Markdown, all my analysis,

00:06:26.780 --> 00:06:29.330
my visualizations, et cetera
and of course, everything

00:06:29.330 --> 00:06:33.290
was controlled through
Git and version control.

00:06:33.290 --> 00:06:36.320
And if you work in kind of a
traditional data role in a data

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:39.830
team that does not sound foreign
or special to you at all.

00:06:39.830 --> 00:06:43.280
But I can tell you that
not all of us are so lucky.

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:47.600
It is true that not everyone
gets the luxury of choosing

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:49.580
their entire tech stack.

00:06:49.580 --> 00:06:53.150
But again, if you work in
a role maybe slightly less

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:55.370
traditionally a data role,
or a data role that's

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:58.010
kind of embedded in a
lot of non data people,

00:06:58.010 --> 00:07:01.580
if you work kind of far from
other tech friendly folks,

00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:06.380
your options might be -- your tech
stack options might be limited

00:07:06.380 --> 00:07:09.050
and not ideal,
which was certainly

00:07:09.050 --> 00:07:10.700
my professional case.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.250
So while going through
a couple of examples,

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:16.010
I'm going to focus
on my two best

00:07:16.010 --> 00:07:17.720
tips for dealing with
that problem, which

00:07:17.720 --> 00:07:22.760
is firstly to always try,
be creative, do as much in R

00:07:22.760 --> 00:07:24.720
as possible, as
less as possible

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.630
in other tools.
And focus on what

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.390
is really realistic
for your situation,

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:32.760
not what's maybe ideal for
the general population,

00:07:32.760 --> 00:07:37.350
someone in a more traditional
data science role.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:39.810
So perhaps you can
access data through R.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.500
I am very familiar
with this problem.

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:44.460
I cannot access my data through R
or I cannot connect directly

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:47.310
to the databases that
hold the data that I need.

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:51.930
I have to use another external
"Oracle-like report studio

00:07:51.930 --> 00:07:54.460
software" in order
to access this data.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:59.430
And so here's how I have finally
decided how I can develop

00:07:59.430 --> 00:08:01.880
a program to help handle that.

00:08:01.880 --> 00:08:03.690
So this is not a unique idea.

00:08:03.690 --> 00:08:07.310
But any time I open a
new RStudio project,

00:08:07.310 --> 00:08:11.060
I automatically set up some
files that I always use.

00:08:11.060 --> 00:08:14.900
And my setup file is
really devoted specifically

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:18.650
to all of my 
report decisions.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:20.900
So again, the report,
the reporting software

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:24.050
through Oracle that I have
to use is pretty powerful.

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:26.240
It allows you to
make calculations,

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:27.060
create new fields.

00:08:27.060 --> 00:08:30.510
You can write SQL, do some
pretty complicated filtering.

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:34.039
But I again prefer to keep
all of that stuff in Ar

00:08:34.039 --> 00:08:38.150
as much as possible so I can
control change more easily,

00:08:38.150 --> 00:08:40.880
reproduce the code
that I want, and write

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:43.919
that code in the language
that I want to write it.

00:08:43.919 --> 00:08:47.910
So within my setup files, I
document all report decisions

00:08:47.910 --> 00:08:50.640
like the name of the report
where the report was located,

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.430
any dates, filters,
et cetera and I also

00:08:53.430 --> 00:08:55.800
make sure that those reports
themselves are pretty

00:08:55.800 --> 00:08:58.000
broad population parameterize.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:08:59.820
I'm mostly dealing
with people data

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:02.820
and I know that when I
want to go to my next steps

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:05.640
and analyze the data, I need to
create new fields and filter,

00:09:05.640 --> 00:09:09.030
et cetera but I try to get the
biggest possible population,

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:11.340
the simplest version
of the report

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.890
that I can run in
this external software

00:09:13.890 --> 00:09:16.350
so I can do again all of
the data manipulation work

00:09:16.350 --> 00:09:21.660
in R -- again where
R is successful.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:23.580
And this is just a
very simple example

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:27.000
of what one of my setup
files might look like.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:28.690
They always start
with lots of comments.

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:32.123
Again, the when, where,
why, how of the report

00:09:32.123 --> 00:09:33.540
and it's all just
convenient place

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:35.555
to again read in all of my data.

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:41.260
You also might need to
Dashboard elsewhere.

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:44.920
If it were up to me, all of
the end products of my work

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:49.330
would either be in R Markdown
or quarto or Shiny or something

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:49.850
like that.

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:52.610
But I work at a
Tableau organization.

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:55.300
I have to use Tableau and
it can be a little dicey

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.893
mentioning Tableau at an R
focus conference, especially

00:09:57.893 --> 00:10:00.310
when I know there's a Shiny
talk going on at the same time

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:01.210
right now.

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:05.380
But it is a necessary tool
that some of us need to use.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:08.080
And any time I
have a project that

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:10.450
involves any kind of
dashboard component,

00:10:10.450 --> 00:10:13.960
I automatically create
a dashboard file.

00:10:13.960 --> 00:10:17.120
And if you're not familiar with
tableau, Tableau actually is.

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:18.940
Have some pretty
advanced capabilities

00:10:18.940 --> 00:10:20.480
when it comes to
dealing with data.

00:10:20.480 --> 00:10:22.300
You can handle
relational data, you

00:10:22.300 --> 00:10:25.540
can look at data at different
levels of detail, aggregations,

00:10:25.540 --> 00:10:28.610
calculations, et cetera
that's all fine and good.

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:30.880
But again, I would
really prefer to do

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:33.340
all of that data
prep work in out

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:34.970
where I prefer the language.

00:10:34.970 --> 00:10:37.870
I have all the I have
all the code in one code

00:10:37.870 --> 00:10:40.180
file that I can much
more easily change,

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:42.460
track, reproduce
rather than having

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:47.680
them scattered all about the
Tableau software as it is.

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:50.740
So again, within
this dashboard file

00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:52.570
that I create, if I
know I have a Tableau

00:10:52.570 --> 00:10:54.730
component to my
analysis project,

00:10:54.730 --> 00:10:56.300
I make sure I have all the code.

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:58.390
I create kind of
extra data files

00:10:58.390 --> 00:11:00.400
at different levels
of aggregation

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:03.580
that I know I might need for
Tableau in any calculations

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:06.070
that I know that I'll need
to incorporate in my Tableau

00:11:06.070 --> 00:11:06.950
dashboard.

00:11:06.950 --> 00:11:08.890
And I also make sure I
save all of those data

00:11:08.890 --> 00:11:12.730
files in a specific folder.

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:14.770
Again, this is a very
barebones example

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:18.100
of what that data of what
that dashboard file are file

00:11:18.100 --> 00:11:19.790
might look like for my project.

00:11:19.790 --> 00:11:23.320
But again, any code to create
anything specific for Tableau

00:11:23.320 --> 00:11:25.570
goes there, and all
the various files

00:11:25.570 --> 00:11:30.100
that I create automatically get
written into a special Tableau

00:11:30.100 --> 00:11:31.660
special folder
usually just called

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:36.770
for Tableau that lives in that
project's working directory.

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.730
So that means that
when I actually

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:42.700
need to open Tableau again,
I just import all of the data

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:44.980
files in that one
specific folder,

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:47.770
and then I can use Tableau
for what Tableau is really

00:11:47.770 --> 00:11:50.180
good at, admittedly,
which is efficiently,

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:53.800
making really beautiful and
interactive data visualizations

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:56.800
that fit with the
IT infrastructure

00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:00.250
at my organization while
leaving, all of the things I

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:02.980
prefer to do in R any
kind of data prep work,

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:04.840
working, joining,
data, et cetera,

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:09.670
leaving that in are where
I can more easily track it.

00:12:09.670 --> 00:12:11.980
And lastly, this might
be shocking to some

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:13.480
of you depending
on the type of role

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:16.930
that you are in, but not
all of us have access to get

00:12:16.930 --> 00:12:19.540
or some kind of version control.

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:22.130
I don't, unfortunately.

00:12:22.130 --> 00:12:26.350
And so within my data
prep, data analysis files,

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:29.020
any files where I'm kind of
writing code, making decisions

00:12:29.020 --> 00:12:34.185
about data, I have a lot of
comments and more comments

00:12:34.185 --> 00:12:35.060
than a lot of people.

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:38.080
But if I don't have access
to the collaboration

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:40.600
and communication benefits
of version control,

00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:44.070
I need to do that collaboration
and communication somewhere.

00:12:44.070 --> 00:12:46.480
And for me, it's just
easiest within my workflow

00:12:46.480 --> 00:12:50.170
to use data comments
in my data files.

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:53.650
So I comment any type of
major decisions that get made.

00:12:53.650 --> 00:12:55.870
And you can also really
just in the Comments

00:12:55.870 --> 00:12:58.670
link to any kind of
supporting documentation.

